---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import Canvas from "../components/Canvas.astro";
import FlowSource from "../components/FlowSource.astro";
import Refusal from "../components/Refusal.astro";
import RunTable from "../components/RunTable.astro";
import Kinds from "../components/Kinds.astro";
import { siteScan, unchecked, checked } from "../lib/flows";

const jobs = [
  "Secondary research on a market: find every major player and what changed.",
  "Comb a thousand documents for evidence and counterevidence on a claim.",
  "Build a model from filings and market data, every cell traceable to a figure.",
  "Find data sources, filter them on five criteria, clean them, populate the model.",
  "QC a finished work product against the sources it cites.",
];

const scanSource = `fn scan(row: Row) -> Scanned {
    let url    = Field::new(url_column).input(row);
    let page   = Source::new(fetch_page).input(url.text);
    let text   = Format::new(html_to_text).input(page.document);
    let issues = Summarize::new(issues_on_this_page).input(text.text);
    Scanned { issues: issues.text }
}

fn flow() {
    let sites  = TableSource::new(sites_to_scan);
    let rows   = Map::new(each_site).over(sites.table).map(scan);
    let report = Format::new(table_to_markdown).input(rows.table);
    let digest = Summarize::new(one_digest).input(report.text);
    let read   = Review::new(before_it_goes_out).input(digest.text);
    let saved  = Write::new(weekly_scan).input(read.text);
}`;

const works = [
  "An agent writes flows in the restricted subset; the checker refuses anything else before it is saved.",
  "The canvas is generated from the source and edits go back into it.",
  "Source (file, URL, document, integration), Search over granted files, Summarize, Template, Format, Review, Write.",
  "Map, Repeat, Refine, Retry and Branch as visible regions on the canvas.",
  "Receipts for every model call, tool call, review decision and write; a run pins what it runs.",
  "Test runs the whole flow and withholds the writes; a finished Test can be escalated to a Run.",
  "A crashed run resumes from its receipts without asking the model again.",
  "Results land in run tables; committing one to the knowledge base makes an immutable revision.",
  "Read-only readers for 21 systems, from Gmail and Drive to Salesforce and Jira.",
  "Personal and governed workspaces; agents hold their own credentials and start Tests only, by default.",
];
const coming = [
  "Origin on every wire, with the knowledge base refusing model-written values nobody checked.",
  "Check: a quote is an exact substring, a value matches a schema, a predicate holds.",
  "Quote, Model, Judge, Compare and Draft as blocks; Aggregate, Join and Reshape as transforms.",
  "Named compositions you insert as one step: Best of N, Adversarial review, Consolidate.",
  "Paste a chat answer and check each claim against the source it cites.",
  "The screens for scheduled and unattended runs, and for requiring an approved revision.",
];
---

<BaseLayout>
  <section class="hero">
    <div class="container">
      <h1 class="display">Hand the work to agents. Keep the process in view.</h1>
      <p class="lead measure">
        Functionary is for research and analysis that an agent could do and
        that you are on the hook for. An agent writes the process as a flow of
        trusted steps. The flow is the program. You read it, run it, and fix
        the step that was wrong.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <Button href="/how-it-works" variant="primary">See one job end to end</Button>
        <Button href="/self-hosting" variant="ghost">Deployment</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="container wide hero-canvas">
      <Canvas
        flow={siteScan}
        run
        status="built"
        caption="A list of sites, one summary per site, one digest, a person, a write. The yellow card is a person."
      />
      <p class="desktop-note">The canvas is a desktop tool. The argument reads fine here; the product does not.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container measure">
      <h2>None of it is hard. Any of it can go wrong.</h2>
      <p class="stack-p">
        The work is the kind an early-career consultant, research assistant or
        analyst does, and AI has been able to do it for a couple of years.
      </p>
      <ul class="jobs">
        {jobs.map((j) => <li>{j}</li>)}
      </ul>
      <p class="stack-p">
        Each of these is dozens to hundreds of small steps. None is difficult
        on its own. Errors creep in anyway, and the person whose name goes on
        the result has no view of what happened below. That is why research
        shops pay for footnote checks and for replications of their own work.
      </p>
      <p>
        Maybe an agent plus an adversarial review loop never gets anything
        wrong. Maybe the mess is fine and the cost is trivial next to the
        benefit. Most people seem to think so, and they may be right.
        Functionary is for the people who are exposed if they are not.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container split">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>You're selling good words.</h2>
        <p class="stack-p">
          In code, a creative fix that refactors something to make it work is a
          help. In research it is how things go bad. An unvetted source or a
          paraphrase that drifted from its quote can contaminate everything
          downstream, and the longer the chain of agents, the harder it is to
          tell whether the one in the middle checked which sources it could
          actually reach.
        </p>
        <p class="stack-p">
          A research organization has no product but its words. When the words
          turn out to be wrong, you trace the step that let it in and change
          the process so it cannot happen again. You cannot do that with a
          free-running agent. You can ask it nicely in a markdown file and hope.
        </p>
        <p>
          Functionary's answer is to mark where every value came from. A value
          a model wrote stays marked through every later step. Only two things
          clear the mark: a deterministic check against the source, or a
          person. More effort inside the model call buys a better draft, not an
          independent look.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="pair">
        <Canvas
          flow={unchecked}
          legend
          status="designed"
          caption="A model-written table goes straight into the knowledge base. Nothing marks it."
        />
        <Canvas
          flow={checked}
          legend
          status="designed"
          caption="A Check confirms each quote is in the filing. The wire after it is verified."
        />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>The agent writes the plan. You read it.</h2>
        <p class="stack-p">
          Agents make it cheap to control a process from above. Instead of
          micromanaging the work, you have an agent write the process out in
          full, then look at it. A plan with holes is easy to spot. A
          transcript is not.
        </p>
        <p class="stack-p">
          For that to work, the flowchart has to be what runs. So the agent
          writes flows in a small, fixed subset of Rust. The checker reads the
          graph off the source without running it, and the canvas draws that
          graph one to one. Loops and branches exist only as the visible
          regions on the canvas. There is no hard-coded data, no file read
          that is not a step, and no second way to execute.
        </p>
        <p>
          Anything outside the subset is refused at save. Until the agent
          writes exactly what is allowed, nothing changes.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="two-up">
        <div>
          <FlowSource code={scanSource} file="weekly-scan.rs" />
          <p class="fig-note">The hero flow, as the agent wrote it. Each binding is a card; each argument is a wire.</p>
        </div>
        <div>
          <Refusal />
          <p class="fig-note">An improvised loop and a bare file read. Both are refused, and the diagnostic says what to use instead.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="measure">
        <h2>Each step is a job you could hand to a junior.</h2>
        <p class="stack-p">
          A block does one judgement: summarize this page and keep track of the
          source text, search the files it was granted, grade this source
          against these criteria. Its prompt is tuned by us and never edited
          by the agent that places it. The model inside can only take the
          actions the block lists. That is the product: being able to say with
          confidence that the only thing this step can do is read its input
          and look at the documents in front of it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Every model call, tool call, review decision and write leaves a
          receipt. A failed row is a failed row in the result, not a blank.
          Test runs the whole flow and withholds the writes, so you read the
          outcome before anything leaves.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="table-wrap">
        <RunTable
          columns={["site", "fetched", "summary", "receipt"]}
          mono={[3]}
          rows={[
            [{ text: "example-vendor.com/pricing" }, { text: "200 · 41 KB", origin: "sourced" }, { text: "Two plans removed since last scan; enterprise tier now quote-only.", origin: "generated" }, { text: "sha256:9f2a1c…" }],
            [{ text: "competitor.io/changelog" }, { text: "200 · 128 KB", origin: "sourced" }, { text: "Three releases; one deprecates the export API.", origin: "generated" }, { text: "sha256:41bc07…" }],
            [{ text: "partner.example/terms" }, { text: "403", status: "failed" }, { text: "", status: "missing" }, { text: "sha256:7d01e8…" }],
          ]}
          caption="One row per site, in the run inspector's terms. The third site refused the fetch; the row says so instead of disappearing."
        />
      </div>
      <div class="kinds-wrap">
        <Kinds />
        <p class="fig-note">
          Seven kinds of step, and the verbs under each. The colour map is decided;
          the canvas does not paint cards by kind yet.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="container measure">
      <h2>Fix the step once. Every later run uses the fix.</h2>
      <p class="stack-p">
        Most flows get built, adjusted, run, adjusted, run, and then rarely
        touched again. That loop carries no versioning ceremony. One person
        creates, edits, runs and reviews, and never sees the word pin.
      </p>
      <p class="stack-p">
        When a flow starts to matter, you ask for friction: pin the tables it
        reads and name who reviews its outputs. Requiring an approved revision
        before an unattended run is being wired in. Every run records exactly
        which revision it ran. A correction is a change to a visible step, with its history, and
        the next run inherits it. The process improves. The model does not
        have to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Scheduled and unattended runs exist on the server today. The screens
        for setting them up come after the rest of this lands.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section status">
    <div class="container">
      <h2>What works today, and what is decided.</h2>
      <p class="measure stack-p">
        The backend described above lives on an integration branch and has not
        shipped. This list is kept honest on purpose.
      </p>
      <div class="cols">
        <div>
          <h3>Runs on the branch</h3>
          <ul>{works.map((w) => <li>{w}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
        <div>
          <h3>Decided, being built</h3>
          <ul class="coming">{coming.map((c) => <li>{c}</li>)}</ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="actions">
        <Button href="/how-it-works" variant="primary">See one job end to end</Button>
        <Button href="/self-hosting" variant="ghost">Deployment</Button>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .hero {
    padding-block: clamp(3.5rem, 8vh, 6rem) var(--section-pad);
  }
  h1.display {
    max-width: 24ch;
    hyphens: manual;
    overflow-wrap: normal;
  }
  .lead {
    margin-top: var(--s-24);
  }
  .actions {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: var(--s-12);
    margin-top: var(--s-32);
  }
  .hero-canvas {
    margin-top: var(--s-40);
  }
  .desktop-note {
    display: none;
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-11);
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  h2 {
    max-width: 24ch;
  }
  .stack-p {
    margin-bottom: var(--s-16);
  }
  .section p {
    margin-top: var(--s-16);
  }
  .jobs {
    margin: var(--s-20) 0 var(--s-20);
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-8);
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }
  .jobs li::marker {
    color: var(--fg-3);
  }

  .split {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
    gap: var(--s-40);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .pair {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-24);
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .two-up > div {
    min-width: 0;
  }
  .two-up {
    margin-top: var(--s-40);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--s-24);
    align-items: start;
  }
  .fig-note {
    font-size: var(--ms-small);
    color: var(--fg-2);
  }
  .table-wrap {
    margin-top: var(--s-40);
  }
  .kinds-wrap {
    margin-top: var(--s-40);
  }

  .status {
    background: var(--bg-1);
  }
  .cols {
    margin-top: var(--s-32);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--s-40);
  }
  .cols h3 {
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--fs-12);
    font-weight: 700;
    color: var(--fg-2);
    margin-bottom: var(--s-12);
  }
  .cols ul {
    padding-left: 1.1em;
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--s-8);
    font-size: var(--ms-small);
    color: var(--fg-1);
  }
  .cols li::marker {
    color: var(--success);
  }
  .cols .coming li::marker {
    color: var(--warn);
  }

  @media (max-width: 860px) {
    .split,
    .two-up,
    .cols {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }
    .desktop-note {
      display: block;
    }
  }
  @media (max-width: 480px) {
    h1.display {
      font-size: clamp(2rem, 1rem + 6vw, 2.55rem);
    }
  }
</style>
